#import "../shared.typ": *
= Conclusions <sec:conclusions>

Crossbot implements the specified validation, normalized thematic locks, dictionary loading, solver selection, verified Unicode rendering and benchmark failure gate. Its algorithmic core is small: shared word-ID masks, reversible domains, equality-specific arc consistency, minimum remaining values, sampled least-constraining words and geometric restart quotas. Forward checking changes one propagation choice while reusing the same machinery. Independent output checking prevents internal-domain assumptions from certifying their own mistakes.

The lineage verdict is conservative. Slot CSPs, propagation, value/variable ordering, bitwise support filtering, trailing, event filtering and randomized restarts are established. The exact bytearray compilation, complement-union choice, signature packaging and sample thresholds have no fully located origin in our bounded search, but this is not evidence of a new principle. We claim an inspectable engineering combination and its evidence trail, not archival novelty or a first-of-kind solver.

The empirical hierarchy depends on the metric. Under the declared five-second, seed-zero, English protocol, MAC filled 87 of 88 files; every verified success was below one second and the worst verified time was 0.271 s. The default misses the required all-grid exit condition on core 87. Forward checking has a lower successful-run median but an additional Gecode timeout; its conditional speed does not outweigh that regression-gate failure by definition. Neither strategy has demonstrated universal superiority.

On the selected three known-filled hard grids and seeds 0, 1, 2, the isolated study is consistent with arc consistency being the strongest reliability/time contributor, followed by restarts and LCV ordering. Signature caching reduced implementation work without changing assignment nodes; degree ties had a much smaller, insufficiently repeated timing effect. Restarts do not activate on most suite successes, and the uncached configuration's identical work count prevents attributing its timing difference to stronger logical pruning. The study also exposes a genuine counterexample: forward checking is better on the measured core 90 seeds. A default can be a reliability choice without being optimal on each instance.

Core 87 is the unresolved bottleneck, not an omitted outlier. Its dense regions and long linking slots suggest a different search structure, but global word uniqueness prevents naive independence. All recorded final attempts across the supplied dictionaries were censored. No witness or unsatisfiability proof is available. A first-moment independence proxy predicts abundant assignments and therefore fails as a calibrated boundary diagnostic. The report makes the negative result visible rather than assigning a convenient difficulty explanation unsupported by a certificate.

The measured position relative to the state of the art is consequently limited: there is no controlled external-system comparison, and modern clue solvers solve a different weighted retrieval-and-fill task. Solver times also exclude input/index preparation; the separately reported load/preparation costs do not substitute for a cold-process full-suite experiment. The repository provides raw seeds, budgets, fingerprints, work counters and successful witnesses so a subsequent comparison can adopt a common protocol.

Concrete next steps are to retain the tested representation/cache invariants, repeat the small-effect measurements, implement a sound generic stronger-search or conditional-region mechanism, and obtain a witness or proof for the outstanding dictionary/grid cases. Only after that should we optimize or learn a strategy selector on declared training and held-out grids and measure cold end-to-end compiler time. Until core 87 can be filled within the common budget, the correct completion statement is partial implementation success with a clearly unmet performance goal.
